Preload libproj + libgdal, and point PROJ at the database, before the extensions.

flet-libgdal ships a shared libgdal.dylib for iOS which links the shared
libproj.dylib, so gdal, fiona, rasterio, pyogrio and pyproj all resolve ONE GDAL
and ONE PROJ. That single instance is what makes both halves of this patch work.

Delivery first: serious_python relocates each extension module into its own
*.framework bundle, while the dylibs stay plain files in site-packages/opt/lib,
and nothing on the relocated extensions' rpath points at them, so the first
import would die with "Library not loaded: @rpath/libgdal.dylib". Loading them
RTLD_GLOBAL in dependency order (libproj, then libgdal) makes dyld bind each
@rpath reference by an already-loaded image's install-id.

Then PROJ_DATA. flet-libproj ships proj.db in opt/share/proj, and on iOS that is
a real directory inside the app, so pointing PROJ at it makes EPSG codes resolve
-- for every consumer at once, since they share the instance. It has to be an
environment variable set before the first import rather than an API call,
because PROJ reads it when it creates its first context. An app that sets its
own PROJ_DATA, or ships its own database as an asset, still wins.

On Android neither half applies: DT_NEEDED resolves the .so by basename out of
jniLibs, and proj.db never reaches the device at all, because Flet's copyOpt
copies only *.so out of a flet-lib* opt/ tree. Both blocks fail their existence
checks and do nothing. Inert on desktop for the same reason.

--- a/osgeo/__init__.py
+++ b/osgeo/__init__.py
@@ -1,4 +1,70 @@
 # __init__ for osgeo package.
+
+# mobile-forge (iOS): the extension modules below link libgdal, which itself links libproj, through @rpath,
+# and flet relocates each of them into its own *.framework bundle while the
+# dylibs ship as plain files in site-packages/opt/lib. Nothing on the
+# extensions' rpath resolves that, so the first import otherwise dies with
+# "Library not loaded: @rpath/lib....dylib". Preload them RTLD_GLOBAL first, in
+# dependency order: once an image is loaded, dyld satisfies each @rpath
+# reference by matching the already-loaded image's install-id. Where a flet
+# build DID relocate one into a framework it leaves a "<name>.fwork" text
+# marker holding the app-relative path, so follow that instead. No-op on
+# Android, where DT_NEEDED resolves the .so by basename out of jniLibs, and on
+# desktop, where there is no opt/lib to find.
+try:
+    import ctypes as _mf_ctypes
+    import os as _mf_os
+
+    _mf_optlib = _mf_os.path.join(
+        _mf_os.path.dirname(_mf_os.path.dirname(_mf_os.path.abspath(__file__))),
+        "opt",
+        "lib",
+    )
+    for _mf_base in ("libproj", "libgdal"):
+        _mf_dylib = _mf_os.path.join(_mf_optlib, _mf_base + ".dylib")
+        if _mf_os.path.exists(_mf_dylib):
+            try:
+                _mf_ctypes.CDLL(_mf_dylib, mode=_mf_ctypes.RTLD_GLOBAL)
+            except OSError:
+                pass
+            continue
+        _mf_fwork = _mf_os.path.join(_mf_optlib, _mf_base + ".fwork")
+        if not _mf_os.path.exists(_mf_fwork):
+            continue
+        _mf_rel = open(_mf_fwork).read().strip()
+        _mf_dir = _mf_optlib
+        for _ in range(12):
+            _mf_dir = _mf_os.path.dirname(_mf_dir)
+            _mf_candidate = _mf_os.path.join(_mf_dir, _mf_rel)
+            if _mf_os.path.exists(_mf_candidate):
+                try:
+                    _mf_ctypes.CDLL(_mf_candidate, mode=_mf_ctypes.RTLD_GLOBAL)
+                except OSError:
+                    pass
+                break
+except Exception:
+    pass
+
+# mobile-forge: point PROJ at the database flet-libproj ships, so EPSG codes
+# resolve. PROJ reads PROJ_DATA when it creates its first context, which is why
+# this runs before any extension import rather than through an API call. Only
+# set when the app has not chosen its own, so an explicit PROJ_DATA or a bundled
+# asset still wins. On Android the file is not on disk (Flet's copyOpt copies
+# only *.so out of a flet-lib* opt/ tree), so the check simply fails and EPSG
+# codes go on raising CRSError there.
+try:
+    import os as _mf_os2
+
+    if not _mf_os2.environ.get("PROJ_DATA") and not _mf_os2.environ.get("PROJ_LIB"):
+        _mf_projdata = _mf_os2.path.join(
+            _mf_os2.path.dirname(_mf_os2.path.dirname(_mf_os2.path.abspath(__file__))),
+            "opt", "share", "proj",
+        )
+        if _mf_os2.path.exists(_mf_os2.path.join(_mf_projdata, "proj.db")):
+            _mf_os2.environ["PROJ_DATA"] = _mf_projdata
+            _mf_os2.environ["PROJ_LIB"] = _mf_projdata
+except Exception:
+    pass
 
 # making the osgeo package version the same as the gdal version:
 from sys import platform, version_info
